WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:08.697
Jean Valjean and Cosette walked for a while and then rode, changing carriages frequently as they headed toward Paris.

2
00:00:08.698 --> 00:00:13.065
The child did not complain even though she was exhausted.

3
00:00:13.066 --> 00:00:21.785
Finally Valjean picked her up. Without letting go of her doll, Cosette put her head on his shoulder and fell asleep.

4
00:00:21.786 --> 00:00:27.856
Valjean had rented rooms in a remote section of Paris, where they would not easily be spotted.

5
00:00:27.857 --> 00:00:35.610
The old Gorbeau house was a ruined dwelling, larger than it appeared from the street and mostly hidden from view.

6
00:00:35.611 --> 00:00:40.252
Valjean unlocked the door, which opened onto a steep staircase.

7
00:00:40.253 --> 00:00:46.840
The first floor of the building was a warehouse, and on the second floor were several run-down apartments.

8
00:00:46.841 --> 00:00:51.177
With another key, Valjean opened the door to his apartment.

9
00:00:51.178 --> 00:00:57.007
The first room was furnished with a mattress on the floor, a table, and some chairs.

10
00:00:57.008 --> 00:01:03.999
Beyond this room was a smaller one containing a cot, where Valjean laid Cosette without waking her.

11
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.404
He bent down and kissed her hand. Nine months before, he had kissed her mother’s hand.

12
00:01:10.405 --> 00:01:16.389
The next morning Valjean stood in the doorway watching Cosette, who was still sleeping.

13
00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:20.737
A cart rumbled down the street, and the noise woke her.

14
00:01:20.738 --> 00:01:30.055
"Yes, madame, I’m coming!" cried Cosette. She jumped out of bed, her eyes still half closed. "Oh, where’s my broom?"

15
00:01:30.056 --> 00:01:36.244
Then she spotted the smiling face of Valjean, and realized she was no longer at the inn.

16
00:01:36.244 --> 00:01:39.714
She saw her doll, Catharine, at the foot of the bed.

17
00:01:39.715 --> 00:01:45.785
She picked up Catharine and played, all the while peppering Valjean with questions.

18
00:01:45.786 --> 00:01:51.848
Where was she? Was Paris a big place? Was Madame Thenardier really very far away?

19
00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:55.952
All at once Cosette exclaimed, "How pretty it is here!"

20
00:01:55.953 --> 00:02:03.769
She was living in rooms that were shabby and nearly bare, but to her this new home was beautiful.

21
00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:07.682
Yet it was difficult for her to shed her old routine.

22
00:02:07.683 --> 00:02:10.255
"Would you like me to sweep?" she asked.

23
00:02:10.256 --> 00:02:14.023
"No, you should play!" said Valjean.

24
00:02:14.024 --> 00:02:23.261
The next morning Valjean sat by Cosette’s bed, waiting for her to wake. Something new was entering his soul: love.

25
00:02:23.262 --> 00:02:30.515
For so many years he had been alone in the world. His encounter with the bishop had led him to be more virtuous.

26
00:02:30.516 --> 00:02:37.269
Now Cosette was making him realize what it was like to love, and be loved.

27
00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:46.420
Cosette was also undergoing a metamorphosis. She had been so young when Fantine left her that she could not remember her mother.

28
00:02:46.421 --> 00:02:50.882
She had tried to love, but everyone at the inn had rejected her.

29
00:02:50.883 --> 00:02:57.150
So from the first day she met Valjean, she began to love her kind, old friend.

30
00:02:57.151 --> 00:03:06.571
Fate had brought together these two shattered beings. Cosette needed a father, just as Valjean needed a daughter.

31
00:03:06.572 --> 00:03:13.888
Weeks rolled by, and Valjean and Cosette lived happily together. He began teaching her to read.

32
00:03:13.889 --> 00:03:21.555
Often while working with her, he would remember that he had learned to read in prison with the intention of doing evil.

33
00:03:21.556 --> 00:03:26.033
Now his greatest joys were to teach Cosette and to watch her play.

34
00:03:26.034 --> 00:03:33.150
Sometimes he hoped that she would grow up to be ugly, so that no one would want to marry her and take her away.

35
00:03:33.151 --> 00:03:38.857
Meanwhile she addressed him as Father and knew him by no other name.

36
00:03:38.858 --> 00:03:43.436
Valjean still wore his threadbare yellow coat and tattered hat.

37
00:03:43.437 --> 00:03:50.857
On the street he was often mistaken for a beggar; sometimes kindhearted women even gave him money.

38
00:03:50.858 --> 00:03:54.608
Valjean always accepted their coins and bowed humbly.

39
00:03:54.609 --> 00:04:02.279
But if he happened to pass a poor creature begging, he would quickly look around and slip some change into the beggar’s hand.

40
00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:08.000
Thus he began to be known in the neighborhood as "the generous beggar."

41
00:04:08.001 --> 00:04:12.157
Only one other person lived on the second floor of the Gorbeau house.

42
00:04:12.158 --> 00:04:17.008
That was the old woman who worked as the housekeeper and managed the building.

43
00:04:17.009 --> 00:04:24.080
She had rented the rooms to Valjean, who had passed himself off as a retired gentleman with a granddaughter.

44
00:04:24.081 --> 00:04:30.255
He had paid his rent six months in advance and said that he was living on his investments.

45
00:04:30.256 --> 00:04:38.159
The housekeeper was very curious about her unusual tenant. She watched him closely and quizzed Cosette about him.

46
00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:45.651
But she learned nothing until one day when she saw him go into one of the empty apartments, leaving the door open.

47
00:04:45.652 --> 00:04:54.484
The woman watched as he took scissors and ripped open the lapel of his coat. Then he removed a thousand-franc bank note!

48
00:04:54.485 --> 00:05:01.810
He soon asked her to get it changed for him, explaining that it was the six-month interest on his property.

49
00:05:01.811 --> 00:05:08.461
A few days later, when she was cleaning his room, she saw his coat lying on the floor.

50
00:05:08.462 --> 00:05:14.382
She picked it up and patted the lining. The coat felt as if it were padded with money!

51
00:05:14.383 --> 00:05:18.735
Meanwhile Valjean never left the apartment before twilight.

52
00:05:18.736 --> 00:05:24.874
His evening walks often took him past a church, where he gave money to an old beggar.

53
00:05:24.875 --> 00:05:33.787
One night Valjean put a coin in the man’s hand as usual. The beggar suddenly raised his eyes and looked intently at his benefactor.

54
00:05:33.788 --> 00:05:42.279
Valjean pulled back, horror-stricken, as the man looked away. The beggar looked just like Javert!

55
00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:48.096
"Surely I’m mistaken," Valjean said to himself. "I must get another look at him."

56
00:05:48.097 --> 00:05:51.519
The next night Valjean looked for the beggar again.

57
00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:55.167
"Good evening!" said Valjean, handing the man a coin.

58
00:05:55.168 --> 00:05:58.582
"Thank you, kind sir," replied the beggar.

59
00:05:58.583 --> 00:06:02.842
Valjean was relieved to recognize the face of the beggar.

60
00:06:02.843 --> 00:06:08.834
"My eyesight must be getting poor," he said to himself and thought no more about it.

61
00:06:08.835 --> 00:06:15.148
A few nights later, he was helping Cosette with her spelling when he heard someone coming up the stairs.

62
00:06:15.148 --> 00:06:19.159
It was late enough that he knew the housekeeper was already in bed.

63
00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:23.801
He made a sign for Cosette to be silent, and then blew out his candle.

64
00:06:23.802 --> 00:06:29.636
Sitting still in his chair, he listened as the footsteps stopped in front of his door.

65
00:06:29.637 --> 00:06:33.405
He could see the light of a candle shining through the keyhole.

66
00:06:33.406 --> 00:06:41.048
Someone was standing outside his door and listening. Several minutes later the person walked away.

67
00:06:41.049 --> 00:06:48.381
Valjean sent Cosette to bed. He threw himself on his bed without undressing, but he could not sleep that night.

68
00:06:48.382 --> 00:06:56.215
The next morning he heard the same footsteps in the hallway. He tiptoed from his bed and peered through the keyhole.

69
00:06:56.216 --> 00:07:02.453
As the man reached the stairs, he turned and Valjean saw his profile.

70
00:07:02.454 --> 00:07:04.396
It was Javert!

71
00:07:04.397 --> 00:07:08.366
Who had given him a key to the building? Why was he here?

72
00:07:08.367 --> 00:07:13.199
When the housekeeper came to sweep Valjean’s room, he asked her no questions.

73
00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:20.612
But she said, "Perhaps monsieur heard someone arriving last night? It was the new tenant."

74
00:07:20.613 --> 00:07:27.332
"Oh yes, I thought I heard someone on the stairs," he said as casually as possible. "Who is he?"

75
00:07:27.333 --> 00:07:33.509
"Monsieur Dumont, a gentleman living on his investments like you."

76
00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:42.326
That night Valjean went out the front door and looked carefully up and down the street. Then he went upstairs again.

77
00:07:42.327 --> 00:07:47.690
"Come," he said as he took Cosette’s hand. "We must leave this place."

